Purely declarative languages have been used for reactive programming for a long time, especially in the **functional reactive programming (FRP)** world. Examples include:

- **Elm** (purely functional, reactive UI architecture)
- **Lucid Synchrone / Lustre**-style synchronous dataflow languages (declarative reactive systems, often embedded/safety-critical)
- **Fran / Yampa** (Haskell FRP libraries; host language is pure functional)
- **Flapjax** and related research DSLs (declarative event/dataflow style)

If you mean “purely declarative” very strictly (no escape hatches/imperative interop at all), most real-world systems still include some imperative boundary for I/O, but the core reactive logic can be fully declarative.

